{"cuisine/mongolian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mongolian-cuisine","n":"Mongolian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Mongolia","intro":[["Mongolian cuisine predominantly consists of dairy products, meat, and animal fats. The most common rural dish is cooked mutton. In the city, steamed dumplings filled with meat—\"buuz\"— are popular."],["The extreme continental climate of Mongolia and the lowest population density in the world of just 2.2 inhabitants/km2 has influenced the traditional diet. Use of vegetables and spices are limited. Due to geographic proximity and deep historic ties with China and Russia, Mongolian cuisine is also influenced by Chinese and Russian cuisine."],["Mongolia is one of few Asian countries where rice is not a main staple food. Instead, Mongolian people prefer to eat meat as their staple food rather than rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mongolian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Borts","dish/borts"],["Buuz","dish/buuz"],["Tsuivan","dish/tsuivan"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Mongolian cooking","count":2,"links":[["Buryat cuisine","cuisine/buryat-cuisine"],["Kalmyk cuisine","cuisine/kalmyk-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3d/Dishes_of_Mongolian_cuisine.jpg/500px-Dishes_of_Mongolian_cuisine.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Bgelo777","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dishes_of_Mongolian_cuisine.jpg"}},"cuisine/maithil-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/maithil-cuisine","n":"Maithil cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Maithil cuisine, also known as Mithila cuisine, is the traditional cooking style of Maithils residing in the Mithila region of India and Nepal. It is a part of Indian and Nepalese cuisine."],["Maithil cuisine comprises a broad repertoire of rice, wheat, fish and meat dishes and the use of various spices, herbs and natural edibles. The cuisine is categorized by types of food for various events, from banquets, to weddings and parties, festival foods, and travel foods."],["The service style of the cuisine has little similarity with that of the French table d'hôte; all preparations are served together on a platter and consumed at once. The staple food is bhat (boiled rice), dal, roti, tarkari and achar, prepared from rice, lentils, wheat flour, vegetables, and pickles. The traditional cooking medium is mustard oil. Panchforan is a common blend of five spices: jeer, ajwain, mangrail, sounf and methi, and is akin to the panchforan of Bengal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"maithil-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maithil_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Tilkor Tarua","dish/tilkor-tarua"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cc/Tarua_of_Aloo_and_Tilkor.jpg/500px-Tarua_of_Aloo_and_Tilkor.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Sntshkumar750","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarua_of_Aloo_and_Tilkor.jpg","of":"Tilkor Tarua"}},"cuisine/culture-of-tonga-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-tonga-cuisine","n":"Tongan cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Tonga","intro":[["The Tongan archipelago has been inhabited for perhaps 3,000 years, since settlement in late Lapita times. The culture of its inhabitants has surely changed greatly over this long time period. Before the arrival of European explorers in the late 17th and early 18th centuries, the Tongans were in frequent contact with their nearest Oceanic neighbors, Fiji and Samoa. In the 19th century, with the arrival of Western traders and missionaries, Tongan culture changed dramatically. Some old beliefs and habits were thrown away and others were adopted. Some accommodations made in the 19th century and early 20th century are now being challenged by changing Western civilization. Hence Tongan culture is far from a unified or monolithic affair, and Tongans themselves may differ strongly as to what it is \"Tongan\" to do, or not do. Contemporary Tongans often have strong ties to overseas lands. They may have been migrant workers in New Zealand, or have lived and traveled in New Zealand, Australia, or the United States. Many Tongans now live overseas, in a Tongan diaspora, and send home remittances to family members (often aged) who prefer to remain in Tonga. Tongans themselves often have to operate in two different contexts, which they often call anga fakatonga, the traditional Tongan way, and anga fakapālangi, the Western way. A culturally adept Tongan learns both sets of rules and when to switch between them."],["Any description of Tongan culture that limits itself to what Tongans see as anga fakatonga would give a seriously distorted view of what people actually do, in Tonga, or in diaspora, because accommodations are so often made to anga fakapālangi. The following account tries to give both the idealized and the on-the-ground versions of Tongan culture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-tonga-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Tonga#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Polynesian Cultural Center Blog: Lupulu","ref":"site/polynesia-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polynesia.com","note":"Recipe and background for lu pulu, the Tongan taro-leaf and corned beef dish.","url":"https://www.polynesia.com/blog/tongan-lupulu-recipe","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Me'a Kai: The Food and Flavours of the South Pacific","ref":"cookbook/me-a-kai-the-food-and-flavours-of-the-south-pacific","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver, Tracy Berno and Shiri Ram, 2010","note":"Regional cookbook with a chapter on Tonga.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/books/OL28452199M","suggested":true},{"name":"Eat Pacific: The Pacific Island Food Revolution Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/eat-pacific-the-pacific-island-food-revolution-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver (ed.), 2024","note":"Pacific Island Food Revolution cookbook with a Tonga section.","url":"https://masseypress.ac.nz/books/eat-pacific","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/40/France_vs_Tonga_2011_RWC_%285%29.jpg/500px-France_vs_Tonga_2011_RWC_%285%29.jpg","w":500,"h":216,"by":"Craig Boyd","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_vs_Tonga_2011_RWC_(5).jpg"}},"dish/appetizing-store":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/appetizing-store","n":"Appetizing store","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["An appetizing store, typically in reference to Jewish cuisine in New York City, particularly Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine, is a store that sells \"food that generally goes with bagels\", although appetizings can also be served with a variety of breads. Appetizings include smoked and pickled fish and fish spreads, pickled vegetables, cream cheese spreads, and other cheeses."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-appetizing-store"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appetizing_store"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","ref":"site/toriavey-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","ref":"site/myjewishlearning-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links":[["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Brent's Deli","dish/brent-s-deli"],["Brisket (Jewish dish)","dish/brisket-jewish-dish"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Challah","dish/challah"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"],["Gaby's Deli","dish/gaby-s-deli"],["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"],["Gondi (food)","dish/gondi-food"],["Gribenes","dish/gribenes"],["Hamantash","dish/hamantash"],["Holishkes","dish/holishkes"],["Jewish deli","dish/jewish-deli"],["Katz's Delicatessen","dish/katz-s-delicatessen"],["Lekach","dish/lekach"],["Liebman's Deli","dish/liebman-s-deli"],["Mafrum","dish/mafrum"],["Matzah brei","dish/matzah-brei"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"],["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"],["Teiglach","dish/teiglach"],["Tsvikli","dish/tsvikli"],["Tzimmes","dish/tzimm